---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Safe RL for Recommendations"
  description="Building Healthy Recommendation Sequences for Everyone"
>
  <article class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
    <h1>Building Healthy Recommendation Sequences for Everyone</h1>
    <h2>A Safe Reinforcement Learning Approach</h2>

    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
      Ashudeep Singh, Yoni Halpern, Nithum Thain, Konstantina Christakopoulou, Ed H. Chi, Jilin
      Chen, Alex Beutel
    </p>

    <p>
      <a
        href="http://www.ashudeepsingh.com/publications/facctrec2020_singh_et_al.pdf"
        class="text-theme-light dark:text-theme-dark hover:underline">Paper (PDF)</a
      >
      &middot;
      <a
        href="https://github.com/google/ml-fairness-gym/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="text-theme-light dark:text-theme-dark hover:underline">Code</a
      >
      &middot;
      <a
        href="/assets/pdf/facctrec2020_singh_et_al_slides.pdf"
        class="text-theme-light dark:text-theme-dark hover:underline">Slides</a
      >
    </p>

    <img
      src="/assets/img/safe-recs.png"
      alt="Safe RL for Recommendations"
      class="max-w-md mx-auto my-6"
    />

    <h3>Abstract</h3>
    <p>
      A key consideration in the design of recommender systems is the long term well-being of users.
      In this work, we formalize this challenge as a multi-objective, safe reinforcement learning
      problem, balancing positive user feedback and the "healthiness" of user trajectories. We note
      that in some cases, naively balancing these objectives can lead to unhealthy experiences, even
      if unlikely, still occurring in a small subset of users leading us to examine a distributional
      notion of recommendation safety. Thus, we propose a reinforcement learning approach that
      optimizes for positive feedback from users while simultaneously optimizing for the health of
      worst-case users to remain high. To empirically validate our method, we develop a novel
      research simulation environment motivated by a movie recommendation setting that considers
      exposure to violence as a proxy for unhealthy recommendations. We demonstrate how our method
      reduces unhealthy recommendations to the most vulnerable users, without sacrificing much user
      satisfaction.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
